  Before raising the order any further, there's a special polynomial that tends to pop up in filter design and analysis.
  It's called a biquadratic polynomial. (Ever heard of biquad filters? -- If not you will soon.)
  Theoretically, a biquadratic polynomial is a quartic (4th-order). It has the form:

    a4 x^4  +  a2 x^2  +  a0        ( a4 ≠ 0 )

  It's of order 4 indeed, but its lack of any odd-exponent term makes it easier to discriminate than the average quartic.
  Define:

    ξ  =  x^2        &&        α2 = a4        &&        α1 = a2        &&        α0 = a0

  It becomes:

    α2 ξ²  +  α1 ξ  +  α0        //  a quadratic in x²

  It has 2 roots for ξ:  ρ1  and  ρ2
  And therefore 4 roots for x:  ±√(ρ1)  and  ±√(ρ2)

  //  Order N

  Cubic, quartic, quintic, sextic, septic, octic, nonic, decic, undecic, duodecic, tredecic... name your price.

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Degree_of_a_polynomial#Names_of_polynomials_by_degree

  Let's define:

    P1 (x)  =  a1 (x - r1)        P2 (x)  =  a2 (x - r2) (x - r3)        //  respectively a 1st- and a 2nd-order real polynomial in factored form.

    Π (x)  =  P1 * P2  =  a1 a2 (x - r1) (x - r2) (x - r3)               //  their product.

  This last expression clearly has three roots:  r1, r2 and r3.
  It also looks very much like a factored form.

    Π (x)  =  a1 a2 ( x²  -  r2 x  -  r1 x  +  r1 r2 ) (x - r3)  =  a1 a2 ( x³  -  r3 x²  -  r2 x²  +  r2 r3 x  -  r1 x²  +  r1 r3 x  + r1 r2 x  -  r1 r2 r3 )

           =  a1 a2 ( x³  -  (r3 + r2 + r1) x²  +  (r2 r3  +  r1 r3  +  r1 r2) x  -  r1 r2 r3 )

           =  a1 a2 x³  -  a1 a2 (r3 + r2 + r1) x²  +  a1 a2 (r2 r3  +  r1 r3  +  r1 r2) x  -  a1 a2 r1 r2 r3

    α3  =  a1 a2    &&    α2  =  - a1 a2 (r3 + r2 + r1)    &&    α1  =  a1 a2 (r2 r3  +  r1 r3  +  r1 r2)    &&    α0  =  - a1 a2 r1 r2 r3

    =>    Π (x)  =  α3 x³  +  α2 x²  +  α1 x  +  α0

  So  Π (x)  is a third-order polynomial.
  Is it real? -- Two cases:
    (1) r2 and r3 are real, then yes it is: the only way to get an imaginary number out of real ones is with a negative sign and a fractional exponent.
    (2) r2 and r3 are complex conjugates...

  Let's try (2):

    α2  =  - a1 a2 ( Re (r2)  -  j Im (r2)  +  Re (r2)  +  j Im (r2)  +  r1 )  =  - a1 a2 ( 2 Re (r2)  +  r1 )    //  ok.

