  So if we use  x - z  as the divisor in the polynomial long division of  P [N] (x)  (the dividend), the quotient will be of order N - 1.
  Let's call it  Q [N-1] (x).
  And the remainder will be of order:  1 - 1  =  0, so it will be a constant.
  Let's call it R [N-1].
  So, based on what has been established during the previous section:

     P [N] (x)  -  R [N-1]
    -----------------------  =  Q [N-1] (x)    <=>    P [N] (x)  =  (x - z) Q [N-1] (x)  +  R [N-1]
            (x - z)

  Right now if we keep running the loop, we risk missing an opportunity to demonstrate a theorem...
  What kind of weirdo wouldn't want to demonstrate every last theorem on Earth? (Answering rhetorical questions is rude.)
  What if...  x = z ?

