    Re ( Z [n] )  =  a [n] Re ( ζ [n] )        &&        Im ( Z [n] )  =  a [n] Im ( ζ [n] )

  and:

    a [n] conj ( ζ [n] )  =  a [n] Re ( ζ [n] )  -  j a [n] Im ( ζ [n] )  =  Re ( Z [n] )  -  j Im ( Z [n] )  =  conj ( Z [n] )

  Then:
                  N            N                                            N                   N                     N                     N
    P [N] (z)  =  ∑  Z [n]  =  ∑  {  Re ( Z [n] )  +  j Im ( Z [n] )  }  =  ∑  Re ( Z [n] )  +  ∑  j Im ( Z [n] )  =  ∑  Re ( Z [n] )  +  j ∑  Im ( Z [n] )
                n = 0        n = 0                                        n = 0               n = 0                 n = 0                 n = 0
  Therefore:
                         N                                                     N
    Re ( P [N] (z) )  =  ∑  Re ( Z [n] )        &&        Im ( P [N] (z) )  =  ∑ Im ( Z [n] )
                       n = 0                                                 n = 0
  Almost there:
                           N                     N                                            N                     N
    P [N] ( conj (z) )  =  ∑  conj ( Z [n] )  =  ∑  {  Re ( Z [n] )  -  j Im ( Z [n] )  }  =  ∑  Re ( Z [n] )  -  j ∑  -j Im ( Z [n] )
                         n = 0                 n = 0                                        n = 0                 n = 0
  Therefore:

    P [N] ( conj (z) )  =  conj ( P [N] (z) )

  Ok.
  Now imagine that z is a root of  P [N] (x).
  It means:

    P [N] (z)  =  0        =>        P [N] ( conj (z) )  =  conj (0)  =  0

  What we've just proven:
  The complex conjugate of the root of a real polynomial is necessarily also a root.
  This is the complex conjugate root theorem:    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_conjugate_root_theorem
  A purely real number is its own conjugate, so the theorem was obvious in that case.
  But all roots (of a *real* polynomial) having an imaginary part are guaranteed to come in conjugate pairs.

  Once we express the polynomial as a product of first-order polynomials and a constant factor, assuming each first-order polynomial is expressed in terms
  of its roots, these complex conjugate pairs will form second-order real polynomials.

  So -- and this is fucking fundamental -- a real polynomial of any order can be expressed as a product of **real** first-order and/or second-order polynomials
  and one optional constant factor.
  Ok.



